# arXiv 2506.10471 — unique partitions of k-trees

> **Status:** the generator and every local G1–G9(c) gate pass, but the required
> external oracle evidence is incomplete. `harden.py` reached OpenRouter and every
> attempted vendor returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. Those calls
> are errors, not model failures, so this directory does **not yet carry a hardened
> shipping claim**.

## Profile

| field | value |
|---|---|
| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
| native domain | combinatorics |
| object regime | finite discrete |
| computational core | graph |
| certificate form | integer tuple (a set partition) |
| native objects | `(q-1)`-tree adjacency list; partition into independent sets |
| intended intuition | change of variables |
| domain essentiality | native |
| reduction | none |

## Problem and trust boundary

The source is Enami, Matsumoto, and Yashima,
[*Contributions to conjectures on planar graphs: Induced Subgraphs, Treewidth,
and Dominating Sets*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10471), specifically Section 2
and Theorem 9 in Section 3. A solver receives a promised `(q-1)`-tree as a full
edge list and must partition all vertices into `q` independent sets. Theorem 9
proves that this unordered partition is unique. The checker needs no theorem:
it checks shape, exact vertex coverage, uniqueness of entries, and scans every
edge for an internal endpoint pair.

Generation is theorem-backed, not solve-then-hide. It starts with `K_q`; each
new vertex is attached to a `(q-1)`-clique and is assigned the one color absent
from that clique, exactly as in Theorem 9's induction. An affine permutation of
the vertex labels then transports both graph and certificate. Presentations on
which lowest-color greedy in numeric label order succeeds are discarded; this
filters a relabelling, not searches for the already-held witness. `verify()`
never reads `inst["answer"]`.

The local correctness and adversary measurements below are reproducible and
pass. The missing bare/hinted/placebo oracle runs are a real trust boundary:
until a funded OpenRouter key completes them, the model-hardness claim has not
been independently established.

## Why Track B, and the easy regime avoided

This is explicitly **not Track A**. A promised k-tree is chordal, so bucketed
maximum-cardinality search (MCS), from the linear-time method of
[Tarjan and Yannakakis](https://doi.org/10.1137/0213035), finds a perfect elimination ordering in
`O(n+m)`, after which greedy coloring returns the partition. At the hard preset
(`n=89`, `q=17`, `m=1288`), the reference implementation verifies on 8/8 seeds;
on the measured seed it uses **11,369 counted adjacency/bucket/color operations**
and a 25-run median of **0.000778 s**.

The no-tool route is shorter. The ordered marked pair determines the first two
terms of an affine cyclic ordering of all vertex labels; colors repeat with
period `q` in that ordering. Regenerating the ordering and advancing its class
counter costs **177 modular updates**. Theorem 9 supplies the uniqueness that
makes this pattern the answer. Supplying the original k-tree construction
order would expose a direct linear coloring pass and erase the compression
test, so the renderer supplies only the graph and ordered markers.

An audit of the unfiltered draft found that numeric-order greedy coloring solved
10 of 1,000 extra shipping-seed instances. The final sampler therefore ranges
over affine relabellings and retains the first one on which that attack fails;
the certificate remains the partition fixed before this presentation filter.

## Worked demo

The demo (`n=8`, `q=3`, seed 0) is genuinely hand-scale:

```text
vertices: 0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7
ordered marked pair: (6,1)
edges:
0-2 0-3 0-4 1-4 1-5 1-6 1-7
2-4 2-7 3-4 4-6 4-7 5-6
```

One answer is:

```json
[[0,6,7],[1,2,3],[4,5]]
```

`verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`. Removing vertex `7` returns
`(False, "partition misses vertices: 7")`. A person can solve the demo by
ordinary 3-color propagation or by noticing the marked affine ordering.

## Difficulty presets

| preset | n | q | edges | role |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| demo | 8 | 3 | 13 | hand example; never ships |
| easy | 43 | 7 | 237 | first oracle rung |
| medium | 67 | 11 | 615 | second oracle rung |
| hard | 89 | 17 | 1,288 | provisional `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` |

No preset has yet been accepted or rejected by an oracle: the first easy call
failed at account authorization before producing an answer.

## Local gate results

| gate | result | measured evidence |
|---|---:|---|
| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted certificates verify (4 presets × 3 seeds) |
| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
| G3 | pass | tagged/fenced prose round-trips; 4/4 malformed replies return `None` |
| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact density `1.18864e-95` |
| G5 | pass | demo has 6/5,796 valid ordered answers; hard reference costs 11,369 operations; 64 restarts cost 261,696 operations and fail |
| G6 | pass | 5 attacks × 8 seeds, 0 successes; reference MCS solves 8/8 |
| G7 | pass | doubling `n` from 89 to 178 grows edges 1,288→2,712 and the certificate space |
| G8 | pass | 60/60 invariance and 60/60 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
| G9(c) | pass | 292 characters, about 73 tokens, 89 atoms, 177 intended arithmetic updates |

The G4 prior is uniform over **labeled surjections** of the `n` vertices onto
`q` nonempty blocks. It already enforces answer shape, exact coverage, and
nonemptiness. The exact numerator is `q!`, because Theorem 9 leaves only the
ordering of the unique blocks free.

## Oracle loop and G9 arms

| arm / preset | completed attempts | solved | outcome |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| bare / easy | 0 | 0 | four retry records, all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
| structural / hard shipping preset | 0 | 0 | four retry records, all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
| placebo / hard shipping preset | 0 | 0 | four retry records, all HTTP 403 key-limit errors |

Consequently `hinted − placebo` is not yet measurable. No conclusion about the
claimed change-of-variables intuition is drawn from `0/0`. All three transcripts
are harness-written and preserve the failed calls; they are not valid hardness
evidence.

## Use

From the repository root:

```python
import importlib.util

path = "results/2506.10471/gen_2506_10471.py"
spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("gen", path)
gen = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
spec.loader.exec_module(gen)

inst = gen.make_instance(seed=123, **gen.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
statement = gen.render(inst)
ok, reason = gen.verify(inst, inst["answer"])
assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
```

After the three oracle runs are complete, emit instances with:

```bash
bash scripts/emit.sh 2506.10471 20 hard
```

## Caveats

- The problem becomes easy for a tool-equipped solver: MCS plus greedy coloring
  is linear time. That is the declared Track B reference algorithm, not an
  omitted attack.
- The compact affine pattern is generator-added structure transported through
  Theorem 9's partition. The graph-theoretic uniqueness is native to the paper;
  the affine presentation is the benchmark's no-tool compression device.
- The five measured attacks are label residues, degree ranks, natural-order
  greedy coloring, 64 randomized greedy restarts, and an anchor-distance
  ansatz. No SAT solver, ILP coloring model, or large-language-model oracle has
  yet produced a completed attempt. MCS is the relevant domain-standard exact
  algorithm and is reported separately, as Track B requires.
- `canonical_key()` uses marked 1-dimensional Weisfeiler–Leman color refinement.
  It is invariant under tested relabellings and separates all 20 unrelated
  samples, but it is not a complete graph-isomorphism canonizer and may collide
  on specially constructed non-isomorphic inputs.
- Zero empirical hits do not alone prove the probability bound. Here the exact
  `q! / (q! S(n,q))` density proves it; the 200,000 samples are the required
  structure-aware measurement.
- The current `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY="hard"` is provisional until bare hardening
  determines which rung actually holds. Do not submit or emit a release from
  this directory before replacing the failed oracle evidence and recording the
  G9 structural/placebo arms.
