=\bigcup_{1 \le j \le k}\left( X_j^+ \cup \left( \bigcup_{1 \le i \le 3}S_{j,i} \right) \right),
\end{align*}
and consider two induced subgraphs $G_k[X]$ and $G_k[X^+]$.
Note that in taking an induced outerplane subgraph,
at most four vertices can be selected from an octahedron graph of $I$, and
at most two vertices can be selected from each $K_4$ of $G_k$.
Note that in taking an induced outerplane subgraph,
at most three vertices can be selected from each $K_4$ of $G_k$.
Thus, we can easily verify that
\begin{itemize}
\item
$|V(G_k)|=22k, \ |X|=14k, \ |X^+|=15k$,
\item
$G_k[X]$ is a maximal outerplane graph in $G_k$,
\item 
$G_k[X^+]$ is a maximal outerplaner graph in $G_k$,
\item
$s_{o'}(G_k) = |X| \le \frac{7}{11}|V(G_k)|$, and 
$s_{o}(G_k) = |X'| \le \frac{15}{22}|V(G_k)|$.
\end{itemize}
Hence, the graph $G_k$ shows that the difference $s_o(G_k)-s_{o'}(G_k)$ is arbitrarily large according to the order.

%%%%%
\fi
%%%%%



